A||||25||lou_dias||Turbos vs. supercharger||||1||
Z||000000||lou_dias||06-16-2000||02:55 PM||||My 87GT has:<BR>3.55L v6 (3.4 rebuilt)<BR>Crane Compucam .423/.423<BR>Magnum Roller 1.52 rockers<BR>Ported intake and heads<BR>Sprint F1 Headers (coated)<BR>hollow Cat conv.<BR>IRM dual free-flow exhaust<BR>platinum 4 way plugs<BR>Accel coil<BR>K&N air filter<BR>Ads Super Chip (Strip verison)<BR>SpiralMax air twister on intake<BR>ripped out the air silencer on the int. tube<P>Estimated hp is ~220<P>I'm looking for a turbo kit.  Any recomendations: Design One vs. IRM Miller/Woods vs. used turbo off of Eclipse vs. RS Motorsports Supercharger?<P>My goal is about 300-330 hp...||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||06-16-2000||05:31 PM||||I might personally opt for the Supercharger.  Firstly because there aren't many supercharged Fiero's out there, and it's nice to have something different. And second:  SUPERCHARGER=NO TURBO LAG||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||06-16-2000||05:33 PM||||I notice you have the Spiral Max "air twister."  Some tests have shown a HP loss from those mechanical wonders. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87PegasusGT||06-16-2000||06:08 PM||||Supercharged. Turbos don't seem to like engines that are bored out for increased naturally aspirated power. I prefer turbos but you probably want a supercharger in that setup.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||06-16-2000||07:29 PM||||Living in the sunny southwest, I could not make the sacrifice of the a/c required by the supercharger, so I would go Design1 turbo. Why? <BR>a. Design1 is located here in Oklahoma City. I've met the owner and his mechaic and they seem like straight-up guys. If something wasn't right with their work, I wouldn't have to go far to get it fixed.<BR>b. Design1 and Miller-Woods is basically the same kit, same designer. Design1, I would assume, has benefitted from evolution.<BR>c. Adapting a turbo from another car has too many ways to go wrong. It is not a simple "bolt-on" proposition. <BR>I like the idea of having someoe else to fix it if it don't work right.<P>Superchargers are nice and do have all those advantages listed by others, if you can live without a/c.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||06-17-2000||12:50 AM||||Your HP estimates are high in both cases.<P>The loss of the AC is a problem, but if that's ok with you then the SC is good. <P>The turbo will produce more boost than the SC but lags. The turbo kits are ECM controlled. One of the kits includes a knock sensor (I don't know which one) and is a must with any significant boost. <P>You compression is likely too high to run more than 6-8 PSI of boost at max and that might get you into the 220 HP range.<P>IMHO<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000006||KnightRyder31||06-17-2000||01:17 AM||||Are you happy with spiralmax? Has it made any performance improvements??? Do you recomend it?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan86gt3.8||06-17-2000||03:56 AM||||I would say a turbo, kina because i think one would work wonders for my 3.8.  Butt get whatever works for you<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||lou_dias||06-17-2000||03:59 PM||||To answer your questions:<P>Spiral Max -- with my ported heads etc... I noticed an immediate increase in bottom end torque and fuel economy.  Driving home from the Fiero Factory after I got my engine done, I was averaging 26mph doing 80+ mph with bursts in the 100+mph range.  Not bad at all when you consider my specs and 50 psi fuel pressure.<P>Turbo lag won't be a problem as I got plenty of bottom end with 214 cubic inches. So tubo lag ain't a problem.  I didn't know the SC kit removed AC so turbo it will be next year.  How easy is it to have the Design One kit installed my a local mechanic?  Are the instructions good?  My compression ration is only about 9.1:1 tops  because the standard 3.4 uses 8.9 pistons and with the over bore and having my deck and heads grinded down just boosts it a little.  I use 89 octane no problems.<BR>I've heard of a Fiero out in Vegas with the Garret Research turbo from an Eclipse pumping out 340hp.  I think it's a 3.4 also.||||reg||1
Z||000009||hugh||06-17-2000||10:43 PM||||Turbo lag can just about completely eliminated by using a dremel grinder on any interconnecting parts(such as the inlet of the compressor)to smooth any bumps or curbs that the air will see as it travels through your system.I have a Grand National as my family car and have no lag when I hit the go pedal.With the turbo you can't run a cam with too much overlap or the cooler exhaust will defeat the purpose of the turbo(that is free horsepower from your exhaust heat) Also ,your exhaust plumbing will require much more work and room.You will need a good wastegate to control boost.Your compression is ok for turbocharging or blower boost as long as you keep it within reasonable limits and use premium fuel.   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||lou_dias||06-19-2000||08:21 AM||||So you are saying I would have to put a working catalytic converter to benifit from the turbo...  I'll need one next year to pass emissions anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000011||phillip||06-19-2000||10:21 AM||||every once in a while in the turbo mags they'll describe a turbo/supercharger combo but i can't remember the company name. belt driven like a super charger but hooked up to the exhaust too. i think it has a clutch of some sort that lets which ever method is most efficient at a particular rpm provide the boost. i think it is most closely associated with watercooled vw engines. might be worth looking into just to get the theory behind it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BBBAD GT||06-19-2000||01:51 PM||||<BR>87PegasusGT:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Turbos don't seem to like engines that are bored out for increased naturally aspirated power.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm curious why you think this?  Is it because the turbo isn't properly matched to the new motor?  Just curious.<P>There are several advantages/disadvantages of both system (and there are several variations of each system, mainly blowers).  The one thing I always stress to people is COMBO COMBO COMBO.  People who first build a modified motor, then slap on a power adder (excluding N2O) without ever thinking it would be boosted have troubles/issues from the start.  They normally have to spend tons of money getting the motor ready to accept the poweradder.  Some people say build the motor around the poweradder, so there wouldn't be any rebuying of parts.  <P>I personally have a Vortech S-trim Blown 302 that pulls 14+lb of boost.  I wouln't tade it for anything.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Sillhouette.com||06-19-2000||02:22 PM||||The turbo supercharger hybrid is built by Rotrax.  That is the unit that RSM uses on their supercharger setup.  It has a turbo housing with the impeller off a production car most likely the Porche (they would not tell us)  and they custom built a housing to run it off a pulley.  Very simple minimal moving parts.  It run at 14000 rpm idle and over 40000 rpm at 5000 engine rpm.  I think it's the best of both world.  You get a highly efficient turbocharger without the famous turbo lag and heat asscociated with the the standard turbos.  Output pressure is your choice with changing pulleys.  <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000014||BBBAD GT||06-19-2000||05:23 PM||||That rotax sounds interesting, but I'm curious how modified the bearing are to withstand the side forces you give the blower when you tighten the belt so it wouldn't slip.  Turbochargers don't have to worry about that.  Don't forget about the crank also, it takes a beating from a belt driven blower.  You can minimize the side load effect with a greater rib belt, or even a cog setup, but I'm getting off the beaten path here.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||hugh||06-19-2000||09:16 PM||||You don't need a catalytic converter to run a turbo.You do need to control the boost with a wastegate or you can develope so much cylinder pressure you can blow head gaskets.In fact you would get more power out of your engine if you could run a dump tube instead of an exhaust system.The turbo actually quiets your exhaust somewhat so you could possibly get by with no mufflers.It seems turbo engines are hard on exhaust systems so stainless is (in the long run) cheaper.I have had 4 systems on my Grand National.Now it's stainless and should last forever.One last thing with a turbo you can get tons of power and still have a docile engine to drive along with good gas mileage.I get over 20 mpg with my GN with 3.89 gears in my 9 inch.   ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Sillhouette.com||06-19-2000||10:26 PM||||See that is the cool thing about Rotrax.  It does not have a shaft going from the impeller directly to the pulley.  It has three roller bearings that orbit around the shaft and the bearings are spun by the pulley. It is hard to describe and picture but a marvel of simplicity and effectiveness.  The way RSM goes about dealing with sideloads is with a tensioner pulley that is installed just above the supercharger on the bracket that holds the supercharger.  <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000017||N_Man||06-20-2000||01:11 AM||||My oppion is go with a supercharger.<P>Driven a few turbo cars but they were all overhead cam engines.  Because of the setup very little to no bottom end but screaming top end.  None that I have driven have been smooth either, they all had lots of turbo lag and poor gas mileage probably the way I drve though.<P>Never driven a supercharged car but ridden in several. WAY better bottom end, smother, and the top end was good.<P>So Id go super because of more consistant power and a smother ride||||reg||1
Z||000018||N_Man||06-20-2000||01:13 AM||||forgot to mention turbos like open exaust.<BR>no cat is alright.<BR>turbos are quieter but running without a muffler is still ultra LOUD||||reg||1
Z||000019||lou_dias||06-20-2000||09:05 AM||||Again, my car has bottom end power.<BR>I don't want to give up AC either or gas mileage when I'm not on the pedal.<BR>Overhead cam engines notoriously have no bottom end.  Even comparing a Northstar to an LT1 you feel the difference in the bottom end.  VTEC Honda engines were supposed to boost bottom end power but I raced a 190+hp Prelude back in 96 when it was brand new and I smoked it by 3 lengths on the 8th with my basic 3.3 stroker and that's before I ported the heads and everything else.  We both had 5 speeds.<P>Looks like it's turbo for me.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mrfixit58||06-20-2000||10:19 AM||||I've been following this thread since the beginning and thought I would chime in with my $0.02 worth.<P>First of all, there are only two types of mechanical force air induction systems: superchargers and turbo chargers. The difference is superchargers are belt driven and turbos are exhaust gas driven. The boost on a supercharger is directly proportional to the motor's RPM via this mechanical connection. The boost from a turbo comes from the pressure developed by the exhaust gasses which, by nature, take time to build (hence turbo-lag). By nature, superchargers provide boost over the whole RPM range while turbos provide boost over a much smaller RPM range.<P>If you take a turbo charger and remove the exhaust gas housing and replace it with a gear driven belt drive, it now becomes a supercharger. This may be done because it's cheaper or easier than adapting a conventional supercharger or because the boost curve better matches the motors characteristics.<P>To provide maximum life to the motor itself, in each case, the motor is usually designed differently. Because superchargers run "continuous" boost, the supercharged motor is generally built with a much lower compression ratio. Or, the motor is built around the supercharger. This is typically from 7-8:1 compression and would be a dog if not supercharged.<P>The turbo motor can be built to provide good bottom end torque (like stock) and a turbo designed to add additional torque/HP at the point where the motor would normally run out of air. <P>Each system has it advantages and disadvantages. Personally, if I decide to keep my 2.8 or build the 3.4 that I've got sitting on the garage floor, I'll most likely add a turbo. If I decide to go with a 3800, I'll spend a couple of extra $$$ and buy the SC 3800.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Roy||||reg||4
Z||000021||BBBAD GT||06-20-2000||04:42 PM||||I beg to differ with what MrFixit58 is saying.  <P>First, the type of supercharger you are describing is a centrifugal type, where the boost is dependent on RPM's (it has a compressor housing like a turbo).  The other type of blowers are the positive displacement type, which differ dramatically from the centrifugal type.  The PD type blowers are the roots/screw type that provide instant maximum boost at a very low rpm (around 2,000), where as the centrifugal type makes max boost at your max RPM.  <P>When I say RPM related, I mean RPM related "when under load".  All supercharges are not ON continously, they only produce boost (the on mode) when they are under load.  You really wouldn't want the tremendous stress on a motor under boost all of the time.  People build lower compression blown motors for the simple reason that they can run more boost.<P>No flames intended, I just don't want people getting the wrong information.  Everybody considering a poweradder should do their homework first, because a poweradder can toast a headgasket or even worse, a motor in no time flat.  ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Sillhouette.com||06-20-2000||04:58 PM||||I need to disagree with both of you.  Not all superchargers have engines built around them.  Case in point is the RSM supercharger.  It puts out 10 psi on stock engine no engine mods required.  The only time I would drop compression is if I use some radical 15 -19 psi (turbo setup).  This will not toast your headgasket neither will a properly tuned NOS setup.  The only time you would toast your headgasket is if the engine has a lot of miles on it with a sudden increase in pressure or you have overheated/unlubricated the egine.  These engines are pretty though (V6) engines.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000023||BBBAD GT||06-20-2000||05:18 PM||||The second part everybody forgets is the part that makes your power adder shine-Timing!  Too much timing and you smoke a headgasket, not enough and you will be saying "where's my power?"  I've seen 50-100 hp swings from just bumping up timing (mostly accompanying by higher octane gas). <P>Want to know why every Blown 5.0 guy knows how to change a headgasket in his sleep?<BR> Mainly becuase he gets super greedy with timing for that last run to get his best ET.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Sillhouette.com||06-20-2000||05:22 PM||||Yep you are right I completley forgot about that fact.   Also I've seen glowing exhaust manifold because of bad timing.  Make sure that if anyone rebuilds your engine they don't forget about the timing marks on the crank. It is easy to forget to replace the marking plate.<P>Later <P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||mrfixit58||06-20-2000||05:37 PM||||BBBad GT,<P>I think were on the same page. Yes, you are correct in calling the belt driven compressor a "centrifugal type" blower (dynamic compressor). It is, by nature, a supercharger... not a roots type (positive-displacement compressor), but a supercharger none-the-less. And you are correct in saying it doesn't' provide boost over the entire RPM range but, based on my reading, it's RPM band is wider and flatter (i.e., less peaky). <P>However, lets not confuse the issue... you still need a belt to turn it and it will produce parasitic horsepower losses. <P>I'm not claiming to a expert by any stretch of the imagination. I'm just paraphrasing what I've read in some turbo and supercharger books. In both books, the centrifugal blower is classified as a supercharger.<P>Semantics....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Roy||||reg||12
